WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.760
We ask a lot of questions
about our diet.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.080
What’s the right way to
treat a chronic illness?

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.720
Fight off a virus,
lose weight?

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.640
The problem is, we get
a lot of different answers.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.920
Well, I’m here to help.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.440
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.680
It sounds like science fiction.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.160
Bacteria in a vial of dirt
taken from a mysterious island

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.200
create a compound
that prolongs life—

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.480
and not just in
the traditional medical sense.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.360
Thanks to advances
in modern medicine,

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.360
we are living longer lives,
but we're doing so

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.880
by lengthening
the morbidity phase.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.320
In other words,
we are living longer

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.160
but sicker lives.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.240
Traditional medical approaches
tend to just increase

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.120
the number of old people
in bad health.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:52.920
Ideally, though, we'd extend
lifespan by slowing aging.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.360
That way we could delay
the onset of deterioration,

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.920
rather than just extend
the period of deterioration.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.920
That's exactly what this
new compound appeared to do.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.720
Researchers called it rapamycin,
named after the bacteria's home,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.400
the mystical Easter Island famed
for its rock-carved figures,

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.640
which is known locally
as Rapa Nui.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.560
Rapamycin inhibits an enzyme
that's come to be known as mTOR,

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.240
or "mechanistic target
of rapamycin," a key modulator

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.600
of aging, characterized
as a "master determinant

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.720
of lifespan and ageing."

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.520
What does the enzyme mTOR
actually do?

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.680
It is the major regulator
of growth in animals.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.760
Activation of mTOR drives
increases in both cell size

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:39.560
and cell number.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:40.760
What's wrong with that?

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.840
The action of mTOR
has been described as the engine

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.520
of a speeding car
without brakes.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.200
In this analogy, aging is
a hurtling car that enters

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.560
the low-speed zone
of adulthood and damages itself

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.720
because it does not
and cannot slow down.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.720
We are over the hill
and picking up speed.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.720
Why don't living organisms
have brakes?

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.600
Because they've
never needed them.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.360
In the wild, animals often
don't live long enough

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.120
to experience aging.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.600
Most animals die before they
even reach adulthood.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.320
The same used to be true
for humans.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.120
Due to early age mortality,
in the 17th century

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.280
most Londoners
didn't even make it to age 16.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.080
So, living beings need to grow
as fast as possible to start

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.560
reproduction before they die
from external causes.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.240
The best evolutionary strategy

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.760
may therefore be
to run at full speed.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:41.920
However, once we pass
the finish line, once we win

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.040
the race to pass on our genes,
we're still careening forward

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:51.040
at an unsustainable pace,
all thanks to this enzyme mTOR.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.840
In our childhood,
mTOR is an engine of growth,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.480
but in adulthood,
it can be thought of

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.880
as the engine of aging.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.640
Nature simply selects
for the brightest flame,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.320
which in turn casts
the darkest shadow.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.280
This is the so-called
trade-off theory of aging,

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.520
a concept technically known
as antagonistic pleiotropy,

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.240
in which a gene can have
a positive effect when young,

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.600
but a negative effect when old.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.520
This explains how genes with
deleterious effects late in life

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:24.920
can persist in a population.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.120
For example,
the pro-inflammatory

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.080
"Alzheimer's gene"
appears to protect

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.760
against childhood infections,
a major killer

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.360
throughout most
of human existence.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.960
What's the downside
of unconstrained growth

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:39.920
fueled by mTOR?

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.240
The enzyme plows
full steam ahead,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.640
revving up construction pathways
to churn out

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.640
cellular building blocks
for new growth,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:50.400
which can include
cancerous tumors,

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:54.120
while canceling
any renovation or demolition.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:55.800
To preserve growth at all costs,

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.160
mTOR actively suppresses
autophagy,

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.760
countermanding
cellular cleansing

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.480
and rejuvenation.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.080
In my autophagy chapter
in How Not to Age

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.280
I explain how this can lead
to accelerated aging.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.120
Conversely, slowing things down
by putting the brakes on mTOR

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:17.040
appears to decelerate
the aging process,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.360
extending life and health.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.520
Inhibiting mTOR is considered

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.680
the best-validated
aging regulator.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:26.440
In our next story,

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:31.560
we look at how Rapamycin appears
to be a universal anti-aging drug,

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.360
extending the lifespan of
all animals tested to date.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.680
The soil bacteria
on Easter Island

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.320
weren't making rapamycin
to slow down aging,

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:42.800
but rather
to slow down the growth

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.800
of its natural enemy,
soil fungi (just like fungi

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.040
make penicillin to wipe
out competing bacteria).

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.040
Fungi from yeast on up,
like all plants and animals,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.960
have mTOR genes.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.720
This target
of the rapamycin enzyme is

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.280
the universal growth regulator
of advanced lifeforms.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.400
So, while rapamycin
originally drew attention

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.360
as an antifungal drug,
we soon learned

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:10.840
it had many other effects.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:12.720
Dozens of published studies
have demonstrated

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.880
that rapamycin,
by slowing down mTOR,

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:18.320
extends both the average
and maximum lifespans

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:19.600
of laboratory mice.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:21.280
In fact,
rapamycin appears to be

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.040
a universal anti-aging drug,
extending the lifespan

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.880
of all animals and organisms
tested to date—

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:29.640
the only known drug to do so.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.480
It can even work
when started in mid-life.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.400
The original National Institute
on Aging Interventions

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.080
Testing Program experiment
in 2009 was delayed

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.560
because the researchers
were having difficulty

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:44.520
keeping rapamycin stable

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.400
in the mouse food
pelleting process.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.360
(It can't just be dissolved
in their drinking water

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.080
because it's fat soluble.)

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:52.480
By the time
they were up and running,

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.840
the allotted batch of mice
were 600 days old,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:59.680
which is equivalent to, like,
60 years of age in humans.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.920
Even though the mice started
the drug so late in life,

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.160
their lifespans
were still extended

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.160
by about 12 percent,
which could equate

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.920
to more than an added
seven years of human life.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:12.640
Initially, it was debated
whether rapamycin

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:14.680
was a true
anti-aging intervention

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:18.720
or "merely"
a potent anticancer agent,

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:19.920
lengthening lifespans

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.320
by just preventing
cancer formation.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:24.200
mTOR signaling is hyperactive

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:27.080
in up to 80 percent
of human cancers,

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:30.600
where it plays a pivotal role
in sustaining tumor growth.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.360
Rapamycin is used clinically

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.960
to prevent the rejection
of organ transplants

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.560
(by suppressing
the proliferation

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.160
of immune cells
that attack the new organ),

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.760
and a peculiar side effect
was found.

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:44.200
In a set of 15 patients

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.360
who had biopsy-proven
Kaposi's sarcoma

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:49.960
(a cancer
that often affects the skin),

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:53.720
all sarcoma skin lesions
disappeared

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:56.120
in all patients
within three months

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.200
after starting
rapamycin therapy.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.520
The cancer just vanished.

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:04.280
As TOR is the master regulator
of cellular growth,

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.880
the cancer remission was not
completely unsurprising,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.160
but subsequent
rapamycin studies showed

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:13.240
it can do so much more.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.840
In animal models, rapamycin
extends healthspan too.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:19.680
Rapamycin can ameliorate
age-related declines

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.080
in cognitive function
and physical function,

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:24.800
prevent hearing loss,

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:26.360
artery dysfunction

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.120
and tendon stiffening,

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:31.960
regenerate the periodontal bone
that holds teeth in place,

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:36.000
and even rejuvenate
the hearts of aged mice.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.040
Remarkably,
health and longevity benefits

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.160
could be achieved
with intermittent

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.880
or transient dosing,

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.520
like getting one dose
every five days

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:47.840
or for just a few months
during middle age.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.880
As a dog dad,
I was excited to read

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:54.200
about the Dog Aging Project,
where people brought

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.400
their middle-aged
companion canines

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.400
to be randomized
to low-dose rapamycin

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.480
or placebo for 10 weeks.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:01.680
Like in the mouse studies,

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.400
rapamycin appeared
to at least partially reverse

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.160
some of the age-related
heart dysfunction

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:09.720
without any untoward
side effects.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.720
Anecdotally, most of the owners
of the dogs

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:13.560
who covertly got the rapamycin

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.240
reported their dogs displayed
increased activity and energy

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:19.120
compared to only a minority
of the owners of the pooches

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.160
slipped the placebos.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.920
It was time to try
rapamycin out in humans,

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:28.840
which I'll cover next.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.680
In Alzheimer's brains
on autopsy,

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.520
markers of mTOR activation

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:36.240
have been found to be
as much as 100 times higher

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.520
than age-matched brains

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:39.800
of those dying
from other causes.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:43.720
That makes sense,
since nerve cells rarely divide

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.880
once they reach maturity,

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:49.040
which would otherwise dilute
damaged cell debris,

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:52.200
autophagy is particularly
important in the brain.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:53.880
Given the "striking" improvement

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:55.640
in cognitive function
in old mice

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:58.360
from the mTOR blocker rapamycin

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.040
and studies showing
the prevention

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:02.600
(and possible restoration
in some cases)

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:05.080
of memory defects
in transgenic mouse models

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:06.880
of Alzheimer's disease,

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:09.400
there have been calls
for clinical trials

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:12.840
of rapamycin
for Alzheimer's patients.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:16.560
After all, mTOR regulates
Alzheimer's biggest risk factor,

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:18.440
aging itself.

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:20.280
The risk of developing
this dementia

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:21.920
approximately doubles
every five years

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:23.600
over the age of 65,

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.120
with the risk of death
from Alzheimer's skyrocketing

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:30.680
approximately 700-fold
from age 55 to 85.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:32.880
Unfortunately,
no such clinical trial

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:34.280
has been attempted.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:36.440
It doesn't even look
like the dementia rates

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.480
of organ transplant patients
given rapamycin

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:41.280
have ever been tracked.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.680
When kidney transplant patients
are initially switched

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.000
to mTOR inhibitors,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.640
they do seem to experience
a significant improvement

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:50.240
in executive function,
memory, and mood,

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:51.880
but that may be in part

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:54.720
because they were switching
from drugs like cyclosporine

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:56.360
which can on their own cause

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:59.360
adverse
neuropsychiatric effects.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:02.200
Rapamycin has been off-patent
for more than a decade;

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.320
so, drug companies
can no longer charge

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:05.640
whatever they want.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.920
Without such price protection,

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:09.600
there isn't
a strong profit motive

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:12.040
to study the drug,
so we shouldn't expect

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:14.920
any large clinical trials
anytime soon.

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:17.720
Another reason
for clinical trial hesitancy

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:18.600
may be the frequent,

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:21.360
and sometimes life-threatening,
side effects,

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:23.920
like immunosuppression.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.120
In one cancer trial
of an mTOR inhibitor,

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:28.360
for example,
two of the eighteen patients

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.520
developed
life-threatening infections,

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:32.480
and one of them died.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:35.160
To fight cancer
or organ rejection,

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.400
we may be willing
to put up with significant risk.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:40.560
But there's
a much lower risk tolerance

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.880
for anti-aging drugs
meant to be used en masse

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:45.400
for healthy humans.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:48.360
The mouse longevity trials
are impressive,

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:49.440
but they're typically performed

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:52.960
in a strictly controlled
pathogen-free environment

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:55.920
that may not translate out
into real-world conditions.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:57.760
The immunosuppressive
effects alone

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.360
are considered sufficient

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.680
to caution against
self-experimentation.

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:05.120
Nevertheless, there is a clinic
devoted to patients

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:07.360
who self-medicate
with rapamycin

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:10.200
in an attempt to slow aging.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:11.960
Rapamycin advocates point out

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:15.320
that the doses required
for anti-aging are much lower

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:18.200
than used in cancer therapy
or organ transplantation

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.800
and, if anything,
might rejuvenate immunity.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:25.200
Is it possible that transient
and/or intermittent dosing

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.320
could also help
reap the benefits

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:29.320
without as many risks?

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:34.240
You don't know
until you put it to the test.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:37.760
Two small pilot studies
of low-dose rapamycin

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:39.320
for a few months
in the elderly

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:41.520
didn't cause
any major side effects

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:45.280
(though did cause diarrhea
in up to 62 percent),

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.000
but also failed to find
any immediate benefits

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.920
to cognitive function
or physical performance.

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:52.680
In 2018, however,

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:55.040
hundreds of individuals
age 65 and older

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:57.240
were randomized
to mTOR inhibition

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:00.480
about 100-fold lower
than what's used in oncology

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:02.160
or transplant patients.

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:03.720
And instead of
immune suppression,

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:06.760
they did appear
to get immune rejuvenation.

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:08.360
Not only did they experience
an improvement

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:10.920
in their response
to flu vaccination,

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:14.080
but they experienced
significantly lower infections

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:17.120
in the subsequent year
compared to placebo,

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:20.480
and they only got the drugs
for six weeks.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:22.560
This suggests
a persistent improvement

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:23.480
in immune function

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:26.600
months after
discontinuing the drug.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:28.600
Still, self-experimentation

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:30.640
continues to be
strongly discouraged

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:33.120
until we have a better idea
of the risks.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:35.360
Thankfully, there are ways
to suppress mTOR

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.200
without taking drugs.

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:40.520
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:43.760
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

